265:
(a) Purpose. Murals and other public art installations are an investment into the historical, cultural, and unique human capital of our city. They capture the unique identity of our city's sense of place and character. They enhance aesthetics, grow tourism, increase business traffic, support tenant occupancy, and helps eliminate the effects of blight. All of these reasons are why proper mural management is supported by the City of Warner Robins.
(b) Application details. The public art application is established to allow for proper review and management of all proposed public murals within the City of Warner Robins' downtown project area. This application will be received by the community and economic development department and reviewed by downtown development authority. Upon review, if approved, the proposed mural or public art installation will be given a Certificate of Appropriateness to allow the artwork to be installed. There will be a forty dollar ($40.00) application fee required before any review to take place. Additionally, upon receipt of a certificate of appropriateness, applicants will have thirty (30) days to install the proposed mural or public art installation. If artwork is unfinished at this time, an additional application may be required on a case-by-case basis. The application will include the following information:
(1) Property owner information including consent for the art installation;
(2) Applicant information (if different);
(3) Artist information;
(4) Information regarding the art or mural itself;
(5) Applicant maintenance agreement.
(c) Definition of mural. Any mosaic, painting, or graphic art or combination thereof, which is applied to a building and not interpreted to be advertising by the downtown development authority. Murals that contain any brand name, product name, or abbreviating the name of any product, company, profession, or business, or any logo, trademark, trade name, or other commercial message are considered advertising signs and must meet the requirements of the City of Warner Robins Unified Development Code.
(d) Definition of public art. Any permanent or semi-permanent structure or item, which is installed for the purpose of investment into the sense of place in the downtown development project area.
(Ord. No. 10-24, § 1, 3-4-24)

